ion of subunit interfaces in the acetylcholine receptor: identification of residues that determine curare selectivity. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>90</volume>:<fpage>9436</fpage>&#x02013;9440.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8415719</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib43"><citation citation-type="journal">Sine, S.M., and J.H. Steinbach. <year>1984</year>. Agonists block currents through acetylcholine receptor channels. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>46</volume>:<fpage>277</fpage>&#x02013;283.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6478036</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib44"><citation citation-type="journal">Swenson, R.P., Jr., and C.M. Armstrong. <year>1981</year>. K<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> channels close more slowly in the presence of external K<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> and Rb<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>291</volume>:<fpage>427</fpage>&#x02013;429.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6264306</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib45"><citation citation-type="journal">Townsend, C., and R. Horn. <year>1997</year>. Effect of alkali metal cations on slow inactivation of cardiac Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>110</volume>:<fpage>23</fpage>&#x02013;33.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9234168</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib46"><citation citation-type="journal">Unwin, N. <year>1995</year>. Acetylcholine receptor channel imaged in the open state. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>373</volume>:<fpage>37</fpage>&#x02013;43.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7800037</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><citation citation-type="journal">van Wilgenburg, H., T. Piek, and P. Mantel. <year>1984</year>. Ion channel block in rat diaphragm by the venom of the digger wasp Philantus triangulum. <source>Comp. Biochem. Physiol. C.</source> <volume>79</volume>:<fpage>205</fpage>&#x02013;211.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6149868</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib48"><citation citation-type="journal">Wen, H., and P. Brehm. <year>2